  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Monday)\nIssued at 1105 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nExtended range model data suggests that a subtle low-level \nstreamline confluence axis (initially positioned across the TN \nValley) will lift northward into the OH Valley and lose definition\nduring the period from Friday-Saturday night, in response to \nsurface pressure falls across the southern High Plains. This, \nalong with SW flow aloft (provided by a weak mid- level trough \ndrifting eastward over the Lower MS Valley), will force PWAT \nvalues back into the 1.8-2\" range, leading to a general increase \nin the coverage of showers and thunderstorms both days. With no \nclear evidence of synoptic scale forcing for ascent, subtle deep-\nlayer warm/moist advection and mesoscale boundaries will likely be\nthe source of unorganized convection (largely tied to the diurnal\nwarming cycle, but with some potential for convection to persist \nat night, as well).\n\nOn Sunday and Monday, there are indications that the Atlantic \nsubtropical ridge will begin to strengthen as it retrogrades \nwestward into the southeastern CONUS. Although this could \npotentially have a negative influence on the overall coverage of \nafternoon convection, low-level moisture return will improve as \nsoutherly flow becomes established within the warm sector of a low\ntracking northeastward into the Upper MS Valley. With dewpoints \nreturning to the l-m 70s, PWAT values will continue to rise above \n2\" and this coupled with collapsing steering currents aloft may \nenhance the risk for locally heavy rainfall and flooding during \nthis period. Due to increased rain chances, highs will fall back \ninto the u80s-l90s, as overnight lows remain in the u60s-l70s.",
